WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.750 --> 00:00:07.750
NITIN MOTGI: Welcome, everyone,
to the Real-World Data

00:00:07.750 --> 00:00:11.150
Integration Patterns
on Google Cloud.

00:00:11.150 --> 00:00:12.220
My name is Nitin Motgi.

00:00:12.220 --> 00:00:15.730
And I'm a product manager in
data analytics [INAUDIBLE]..

00:00:15.730 --> 00:00:19.430
So before we get started on the
patterns of data integration

00:00:19.430 --> 00:00:22.610
in Google Cloud, let's look
at what the traditional view

00:00:22.610 --> 00:00:24.830
of data integration is.

00:00:24.830 --> 00:00:27.740
This traditional view of data
integration-- data integration

00:00:27.740 --> 00:00:31.400
is such a prevalent
capability that

00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:35.330
exists across of the wide
variety of industries.

00:00:35.330 --> 00:00:38.510
And it's used horizontally in
various different applications.

00:00:38.510 --> 00:00:41.450
From a data perspective, what
data integration provides

00:00:41.450 --> 00:00:45.550
is a one-stop shop for
handling a batch of data.

00:00:45.550 --> 00:00:49.760
And it does provide, as the
word was "batch" originally,

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:53.430
so it does provide a robust
batch handling of the data.

00:00:53.430 --> 00:00:56.120
It has a hardened
orchestration capabilities.

00:00:56.120 --> 00:00:59.300
And most important of
all, it has a wide variety

00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:00.020
of connectors.

00:01:00.020 --> 00:01:05.375
Whether you are connecting
it to the filers, mainframes,

00:01:05.375 --> 00:01:07.940
whether you're connecting
talking to databases

00:01:07.940 --> 00:01:10.410
or warehouses, a wide
variety of connectors

00:01:10.410 --> 00:01:12.410
exists, not only in terms
of [INAUDIBLE] systems

00:01:12.410 --> 00:01:16.010
they can connect to but also
various different formats that

00:01:16.010 --> 00:01:19.800
can be processed
using these tools.

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:23.200
So the best thing about a
data integration solution

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:25.750
are the utility of the
tool that provides it is

00:01:25.750 --> 00:01:28.700
the user-friendly environment.

00:01:28.700 --> 00:01:30.190
All of the data
integration tools

00:01:30.190 --> 00:01:34.610
provide you a user interface, a
visual development environment

00:01:34.610 --> 00:01:36.290
for building these pipelines.

00:01:36.290 --> 00:01:39.680
You can think about
any tool that you

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:41.750
take from a data
integration perspective

00:01:41.750 --> 00:01:43.820
has that studio,
which can be used

00:01:43.820 --> 00:01:46.830
for building ETL pipelines.

00:01:46.830 --> 00:01:48.770
And that is, you
can drag and drop.

00:01:48.770 --> 00:01:50.680
You can drop different
components in there

00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:51.662
and figure out them.

00:01:51.662 --> 00:01:52.370
And there you go.

00:01:52.370 --> 00:01:56.450
You have a much easier
way of moving data around.

00:01:56.450 --> 00:01:59.600
And the one thing
that is very clear,

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:03.290
after looking at being in this
industry for quite some time

00:02:03.290 --> 00:02:05.760
now, is they are optimized.

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:07.610
What I find is they
are more optimized

00:02:07.610 --> 00:02:09.280
for on-prem environment.

00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:13.130
An on-prem environment, it's
varied from a fixed processing

00:02:13.130 --> 00:02:14.160
capability.

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:17.180
And then you look at it-- the
amount of optimization that

00:02:17.180 --> 00:02:18.950
goes in essentially--

00:02:18.950 --> 00:02:21.610
and amount of data
that you can handle.

00:02:21.610 --> 00:02:25.850
It's very much controlled and
limited in some sense, right?

00:02:25.850 --> 00:02:30.790
But what is happening
is in the era of cloud,

00:02:30.790 --> 00:02:33.300
data integration still
plays a very vital role.

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:37.440
There are a lot of benefits
that data integration provides.

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:40.520
And then the thing is,
it enables businesses

00:02:40.520 --> 00:02:43.217
to have various different--

00:02:43.217 --> 00:02:45.050
[INAUDIBLE] enable
various different aspects

00:02:45.050 --> 00:02:45.720
of the business.

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:47.270
One thing I would
like to talk about

00:02:47.270 --> 00:02:49.580
is faster time to insights.

00:02:49.580 --> 00:02:52.640
Having a much easier
way of specifying

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:55.310
how your transformation is
providing a visual environment

00:02:55.310 --> 00:02:58.160
to [INAUDIBLE],, then you don't
have to write a lot of code

00:02:58.160 --> 00:03:00.830
and then manage it and
then just evolve it

00:03:00.830 --> 00:03:02.070
over a period of time.

00:03:02.070 --> 00:03:04.310
But having a standardized
way of doing that

00:03:04.310 --> 00:03:05.490
is extremely important.

00:03:05.490 --> 00:03:10.400
And this allows customers to
have faster time to insights.

00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:12.170
Now because it's
a standardized way

00:03:12.170 --> 00:03:14.470
of specifying transformations
in a visual environment

00:03:14.470 --> 00:03:17.542
for providing all of
the transformations as

00:03:17.542 --> 00:03:20.000
[INAUDIBLE] to change things
that are required for movement

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:20.960
of the data.

00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:24.530
And operationally, it provides
a standard [INAUDIBLE] way

00:03:24.530 --> 00:03:26.090
for monitoring these things.

00:03:26.090 --> 00:03:28.520
It provides lower [INAUDIBLE].

00:03:28.520 --> 00:03:32.508
And because of its simplicity,
you don't have to be an expert.

00:03:32.508 --> 00:03:34.800
You don't have to understand
[INAUDIBLE] to actually go

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:37.280
ahead and use this tool.

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:39.630
It does provides
wider accessibility.

00:03:39.630 --> 00:03:42.450
Now a lot of business
analysts or data analysts

00:03:42.450 --> 00:03:46.770
can actually use this tool to
apply and move transformations

00:03:46.770 --> 00:03:49.200
and move the data around
in their organization

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:54.240
for their business reporting
or analytics leads.

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:59.627
Because of the
ability to, again--

00:03:59.627 --> 00:04:00.960
the simplicity that it provides.

00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:04.440
I would say simplicity
that it provides and also

00:04:04.440 --> 00:04:07.140
the consistency in
[INAUDIBLE] that it provides

00:04:07.140 --> 00:04:09.530
in terms of how you
actually move data around,

00:04:09.530 --> 00:04:14.190
how you do data manipulation and
standardization and cleansing.

00:04:14.190 --> 00:04:17.160
It provides
operational efficiency.

00:04:17.160 --> 00:04:19.500
And the best part of any
data integration solution

00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:21.600
is while you are
in at point A, you

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:23.850
are in a development
environment.

00:04:23.850 --> 00:04:26.583
You're developing the pipeline
or designing the pipeline.

00:04:26.583 --> 00:04:28.500
Immediately, you can
think what you have build

00:04:28.500 --> 00:04:30.990
and translate that
operationally,

00:04:30.990 --> 00:04:34.050
being able to run that in
a production environment.

00:04:34.050 --> 00:04:37.650
And being able to monitor
that in a consistent way helps

00:04:37.650 --> 00:04:38.610
the DevOps team--

00:04:38.610 --> 00:04:41.710
DevOps teams have to just
once what they need to do.

00:04:41.710 --> 00:04:44.950
And they're able to take all
of that, the learnings from one

00:04:44.950 --> 00:04:46.770
set of pipeline, and
apply the same thing

00:04:46.770 --> 00:04:50.350
to other pipeline that get
developed as part of this.

00:04:50.350 --> 00:04:56.880
Now because an integration
tool talks to a lot of systems,

00:04:56.880 --> 00:04:59.080
whether it's systems
or sources or formats.

00:04:59.080 --> 00:05:00.250
It doesn't matter.

00:05:00.250 --> 00:05:02.290
They're able to capture
a lot of metadata.

00:05:02.290 --> 00:05:04.240
And that metadata--
whether it is technical,

00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:05.740
business, and
operational metadata--

00:05:05.740 --> 00:05:06.820
is extremely important.

00:05:06.820 --> 00:05:10.030
And it provides a lot of
insights into the data.

00:05:10.030 --> 00:05:11.860
And these are some
of the benefits

00:05:11.860 --> 00:05:17.350
that data integration
basically enables.

00:05:17.350 --> 00:05:20.610
Without these, it
becomes very hard

00:05:20.610 --> 00:05:25.120
when you are actually looking
to move, migrate, and evolve

00:05:25.120 --> 00:05:28.690
the data strategy that you
have within your organization.

00:05:28.690 --> 00:05:31.030
But none of these, when you
look at the traditional view

00:05:31.030 --> 00:05:34.420
and the benefits that
these tools provide,

00:05:34.420 --> 00:05:38.320
with the new era, cloud
era, you are actually

00:05:38.320 --> 00:05:39.730
seeing a lot more challenges.

00:05:39.730 --> 00:05:42.440
In the current world, you're
seeing a lot more challenges.

00:05:42.440 --> 00:05:45.530
And these challenges can be
classified into three parts.

00:05:45.530 --> 00:05:47.560
One is the growing data assets.

00:05:47.560 --> 00:05:48.570
You're all aware.

00:05:48.570 --> 00:05:50.260
There is more data everywhere.

00:05:50.260 --> 00:05:53.360
You're not able to actually get
a handle on all of the data.

00:05:53.360 --> 00:05:55.660
If you are getting
access to that data,

00:05:55.660 --> 00:05:57.670
you just don't know
how we can bring them

00:05:57.670 --> 00:05:59.890
all together to
further your analytics

00:05:59.890 --> 00:06:01.360
or for your business reporting.

00:06:01.360 --> 00:06:02.350
It's very hard.

00:06:02.350 --> 00:06:05.060
You would have to
do ad hoc one time

00:06:05.060 --> 00:06:07.968
at point-to-point integrations.

00:06:07.968 --> 00:06:10.010
Whether you're doing it
manually, do it yourself,

00:06:10.010 --> 00:06:11.140
it doesn't matter.

00:06:11.140 --> 00:06:13.880
The whole idea here is it takes
a lot of effort to do that.

00:06:13.880 --> 00:06:17.110
And as the data assets
grow exponentially,

00:06:17.110 --> 00:06:20.580
it makes it extremely
difficult to bring data

00:06:20.580 --> 00:06:25.060
from various different
silos, or environments,

00:06:25.060 --> 00:06:26.290
into one single place.

00:06:26.290 --> 00:06:28.380
That's a huge
challenge right now.

00:06:28.380 --> 00:06:31.780
Now adding to that challenge is
the complexity of environments.

00:06:31.780 --> 00:06:33.275
Now we were talking about--

00:06:33.275 --> 00:06:35.260
before, we used to talk
about everything being

00:06:35.260 --> 00:06:36.730
on-prem behind the firewall.

00:06:36.730 --> 00:06:37.780
Life is good.

00:06:37.780 --> 00:06:39.450
But now, it's not the case.

00:06:39.450 --> 00:06:40.760
You have on-prem.

00:06:40.760 --> 00:06:42.590
You have your own data centers.

00:06:42.590 --> 00:06:43.980
Then you have private cloud.

00:06:43.980 --> 00:06:45.620
And then you have public clouds.

00:06:45.620 --> 00:06:47.290
Now the public
cloud's coming in.

00:06:47.290 --> 00:06:49.090
Not everything can
move in the public.

00:06:49.090 --> 00:06:50.470
You have hybrid.

00:06:50.470 --> 00:06:52.650
And people also adopt
multiple clouds.

00:06:52.650 --> 00:06:55.690
So there's some data in cloud
A, some data in cloud B.

00:06:55.690 --> 00:06:58.360
So your data assets
are spread across.

00:06:58.360 --> 00:07:00.670
Now the complexity
is, how do you

00:07:00.670 --> 00:07:03.650
manage data that's spread across
various different environments.

00:07:03.650 --> 00:07:06.420
It's extremely difficult.
You use five different tools.

00:07:06.420 --> 00:07:10.720
Or you choose to basically do
it all by yourself individually

00:07:10.720 --> 00:07:13.030
for each of these environments.

00:07:13.030 --> 00:07:17.040
It's just
incomprehensible for you

00:07:17.040 --> 00:07:18.880
to basically take
all of these things

00:07:18.880 --> 00:07:21.640
and bring them
together seamlessly.

00:07:21.640 --> 00:07:25.090
So this is essentially
leading to a lot more problems

00:07:25.090 --> 00:07:27.670
and challenges from a data
integration perspective.

00:07:27.670 --> 00:07:31.010
And these are some of the
things that traditionally

00:07:31.010 --> 00:07:34.390
data integration tools
have to solve for now.

00:07:34.390 --> 00:07:38.140
The last one is because
there is so much data

00:07:38.140 --> 00:07:40.420
that is being ingested,
there's a lot more metadata

00:07:40.420 --> 00:07:41.680
that's being aggregated.

00:07:41.680 --> 00:07:43.570
All of the metadata
that is being aggregated

00:07:43.570 --> 00:07:44.850
is passive right now.

00:07:44.850 --> 00:07:47.770
There is no intelligence
based on that amount of data

00:07:47.770 --> 00:07:49.620
to actually leverage
that metadata

00:07:49.620 --> 00:07:53.340
to make better decisions of
building smarter pipelines.

00:07:53.340 --> 00:07:56.915
So essentially, figuring
out how to execute

00:07:56.915 --> 00:07:57.940
in a much better way.

00:07:57.940 --> 00:08:01.030
All of these type of
things are something

00:08:01.030 --> 00:08:04.510
that the traditional
view misses.

00:08:04.510 --> 00:08:07.738
Now that we have looked at
what a traditional view is,

00:08:07.738 --> 00:08:10.280
what are the benefits of having
a data integration solution--

00:08:10.280 --> 00:08:12.630
and when we look at the
challenges that it presents,

00:08:12.630 --> 00:08:14.380
what should a North
Star data integration

00:08:14.380 --> 00:08:16.430
solution should look like?

00:08:16.430 --> 00:08:17.210
It's very simple.

00:08:17.210 --> 00:08:19.600
I have simplified this picture
into-- there are so many

00:08:19.600 --> 00:08:21.130
different aspects of it.

00:08:21.130 --> 00:08:22.780
But I have tried
simplifying and tried

00:08:22.780 --> 00:08:25.120
to focus on core
components that need

00:08:25.120 --> 00:08:27.220
to be existing in
a data integration

00:08:27.220 --> 00:08:29.210
solution for future.

00:08:29.210 --> 00:08:32.380
So this is where we
are headed towards--

00:08:32.380 --> 00:08:34.510
first of all, any data
integration solution

00:08:34.510 --> 00:08:37.733
should basically support
different environments

00:08:37.733 --> 00:08:39.400
that it can run on,
whether it's hybrid,

00:08:39.400 --> 00:08:41.480
multicloud, whether it's GCP.

00:08:41.480 --> 00:08:42.440
It doesn't matter.

00:08:42.440 --> 00:08:45.220
It should be able to
support seamlessly running

00:08:45.220 --> 00:08:47.740
on all these environments.

00:08:47.740 --> 00:08:51.190
Second, it should
have seamless ability

00:08:51.190 --> 00:08:54.240
for aggregating metadata from
various different sources.

00:08:54.240 --> 00:08:56.650
So from all the sources
that it interacts with,

00:08:56.650 --> 00:08:59.470
it has to aggregate all
of that metadata without--

00:08:59.470 --> 00:09:00.590
it should be hassle free.

00:09:00.590 --> 00:09:03.670
It should not be someone has
to spend a huge amount of time

00:09:03.670 --> 00:09:05.280
to bring that data in.

00:09:05.280 --> 00:09:09.580
Once the metadata comes in, the
metadata has to be activated.

00:09:09.580 --> 00:09:11.890
[INAUDIBLE] activating
that data that means we

00:09:11.890 --> 00:09:15.190
are essentially gathering
insights from that metadata,

00:09:15.190 --> 00:09:20.260
understanding how different
entities and the semantics

00:09:20.260 --> 00:09:22.050
and the relationships exist.

00:09:22.050 --> 00:09:24.580
Understand more in depth
about the characteristics

00:09:24.580 --> 00:09:25.510
of the data.

00:09:25.510 --> 00:09:30.730
Once you know more about it,
[INAUDIBLE] activated metadata

00:09:30.730 --> 00:09:32.830
in terms of automating
integrations.

00:09:32.830 --> 00:09:35.030
So you will then have
recommendations probably.

00:09:35.030 --> 00:09:36.850
You will make changes to it.

00:09:36.850 --> 00:09:41.440
In terms of assisting the
development of the pipelines

00:09:41.440 --> 00:09:44.170
that the are building, actually,
all those types of things

00:09:44.170 --> 00:09:46.180
become extremely important.

00:09:46.180 --> 00:09:48.370
And the one thing
that you will always

00:09:48.370 --> 00:09:50.890
think about, from a
North Star perspective,

00:09:50.890 --> 00:09:52.540
from a data
integration solution,

00:09:52.540 --> 00:09:54.100
is the ability to orchestrate.

00:09:54.100 --> 00:09:55.735
So orchestration
is a complex piece.

00:09:55.735 --> 00:09:57.610
And when you think about
orchestration, It's.

00:09:57.610 --> 00:09:59.440
Not just about
orchestration or pipelines.

00:09:59.440 --> 00:10:01.510
So there are several
different components

00:10:01.510 --> 00:10:05.110
that need to be orchestrated
that are really important.

00:10:05.110 --> 00:10:08.500
And that is something the
orchestration capability

00:10:08.500 --> 00:10:09.730
needs to be providing.

00:10:09.730 --> 00:10:13.660
In addition to that,
the most important--

00:10:13.660 --> 00:10:17.140
and also one of the
important things

00:10:17.140 --> 00:10:20.420
from our perspective
of manageability--

00:10:20.420 --> 00:10:22.130
is data operations,
having the ability

00:10:22.130 --> 00:10:27.350
to manage the pipelines,
understanding the SLAs,

00:10:27.350 --> 00:10:28.940
ability to monitor
them constantly

00:10:28.940 --> 00:10:30.240
is extremely important.

00:10:30.240 --> 00:10:33.500
So when you think about it from
all of these capabilities that

00:10:33.500 --> 00:10:37.760
needs to be existing in
our ideal data integration

00:10:37.760 --> 00:10:40.700
solution, you can think
about this being a more

00:10:40.700 --> 00:10:42.430
integrated platform.

00:10:42.430 --> 00:10:44.320
With comprehensive
set of capabilities,

00:10:44.320 --> 00:10:46.280
whether you are
actually moving data

00:10:46.280 --> 00:10:50.940
in bulk, batch, real time, or
streaming, it doesn't matter.

00:10:50.940 --> 00:10:53.820
It also has to
provide deep transfer

00:10:53.820 --> 00:10:55.700
smart transformational
capabilities.

00:10:55.700 --> 00:10:58.550
It should support
replicating data, whether--

00:10:58.550 --> 00:11:01.150
in real-time replication
or batch replication.

00:11:01.150 --> 00:11:04.740
Or it's basically snapshotted.

00:11:04.740 --> 00:11:07.350
But the ability to
do that in chunks,

00:11:07.350 --> 00:11:09.600
those will always be supported.

00:11:09.600 --> 00:11:14.570
And most importantly, a
data integration solution

00:11:14.570 --> 00:11:18.710
should provide the ability to
improve the quality of the data

00:11:18.710 --> 00:11:22.760
as you basically go into
the integration process.

00:11:22.760 --> 00:11:27.757
It should not be that
integration process, actually,

00:11:27.757 --> 00:11:29.090
reduces the quality of the data.

00:11:29.090 --> 00:11:31.370
It should actually make
it easy for improving

00:11:31.370 --> 00:11:32.850
the quality of the data.

00:11:32.850 --> 00:11:34.760
And that's something
that we believe

00:11:34.760 --> 00:11:38.780
at North Star, a hypothetical
data integration solution,

00:11:38.780 --> 00:11:41.100
should be providing.

00:11:41.100 --> 00:11:43.590
So now that we have looked at
what are the key things that

00:11:43.590 --> 00:11:47.540
need to be existing in the North
Star data integration solution,

00:11:47.540 --> 00:11:50.450
let's compare that with
what we have in GCP.

00:11:50.450 --> 00:11:54.557
And while we have all
of the ingredients,

00:11:54.557 --> 00:11:56.140
there are a lot of
missing pieces too.

00:11:56.140 --> 00:11:58.760
And we'll talk about
what we have today

00:11:58.760 --> 00:12:01.810
and where we want to see,
in the future, the data

00:12:01.810 --> 00:12:05.200
integration to [INAUDIBLE].

00:12:05.200 --> 00:12:07.400
So what do we have?

00:12:07.400 --> 00:12:11.483
We have five or six components
that are already available.

00:12:11.483 --> 00:12:12.650
Let's go through them first.

00:12:12.650 --> 00:12:14.260
It's one is Data Catalog.

00:12:14.260 --> 00:12:16.600
And it's basically
a simple catalog

00:12:16.600 --> 00:12:18.105
for storing all of the metadata.

00:12:18.105 --> 00:12:20.230
Then you have Composer,
which provides the workflow

00:12:20.230 --> 00:12:21.240
management.

00:12:21.240 --> 00:12:24.280
You have Fusion, which
is the integration

00:12:24.280 --> 00:12:25.780
solution or service.

00:12:25.780 --> 00:12:27.950
Then you have Dataflow
and Data Processing,

00:12:27.950 --> 00:12:34.540
which are essentially
transformational engines that

00:12:34.540 --> 00:12:36.390
are used for
processing your data.

00:12:36.390 --> 00:12:38.020
And then you have
Dataprep, which

00:12:38.020 --> 00:12:40.820
is [INAUDIBLE]
Trifecta, which provides

00:12:40.820 --> 00:12:42.230
the [INAUDIBLE] capability.

00:12:42.230 --> 00:12:45.280
So these are all six
components, six services

00:12:45.280 --> 00:12:50.150
that have integral part of
an integration solution.

00:12:50.150 --> 00:12:53.270
So we look at some of
the capabilities that

00:12:53.270 --> 00:12:56.660
exist across all of these that
will enable us to have a better

00:12:56.660 --> 00:12:58.460
integration--

00:12:58.460 --> 00:13:00.920
a good integration
solution on GCP.

00:13:00.920 --> 00:13:03.260
So let's dive
deeper to understand

00:13:03.260 --> 00:13:07.500
what are the different
capabilities that we have here.

00:13:07.500 --> 00:13:11.465
So first, in order to
have data integration

00:13:11.465 --> 00:13:12.840
for optimized
analytics, you need

00:13:12.840 --> 00:13:14.790
to be able to have
faster time to insight.

00:13:14.790 --> 00:13:15.840
And what does that mean?

00:13:15.840 --> 00:13:18.780
Ability to support broad set
of connectors and formats.

00:13:18.780 --> 00:13:21.310
I should be able to extract
data from databases,

00:13:21.310 --> 00:13:25.950
SAS sources, cloud,
even mainframes,

00:13:25.950 --> 00:13:27.700
various different components.

00:13:27.700 --> 00:13:29.650
We should be able to
pull that data in.

00:13:29.650 --> 00:13:31.680
And not only that, but
we are able to connect

00:13:31.680 --> 00:13:33.180
to various different
source systems.

00:13:33.180 --> 00:13:34.470
You should also
be able to support

00:13:34.470 --> 00:13:35.553
various different formats.

00:13:35.553 --> 00:13:40.760
So you should have JSON, CSV,
XML, HL7, those kind of things.

00:13:40.760 --> 00:13:44.180
[INAUDIBLE] the next thing
is, for our data integration

00:13:44.180 --> 00:13:47.120
solution-- so we do have in
Data Fusion the ability to have

00:13:47.120 --> 00:13:48.880
a code-free environment--

00:13:48.880 --> 00:13:49.610
and with code.

00:13:49.610 --> 00:13:52.350
So you can write code, or
you don't need to write code.

00:13:52.350 --> 00:13:54.410
So you can build
the transformations

00:13:54.410 --> 00:13:56.300
and prepare all your
data without having

00:13:56.300 --> 00:13:59.140
to write any code.

00:13:59.140 --> 00:14:02.567
Just with your mouse,
you'll be able to bring data

00:14:02.567 --> 00:14:04.400
from various different
sources, transform it

00:14:04.400 --> 00:14:07.550
without having to
absolutely write any code.

00:14:07.550 --> 00:14:09.380
Now once you write
this code, the thing

00:14:09.380 --> 00:14:11.060
is, you need to have the
ability to [INAUDIBLE]..

00:14:11.060 --> 00:14:12.560
While you're doing
functional tests,

00:14:12.560 --> 00:14:15.110
you are trying to understand
whether the logic that you

00:14:15.110 --> 00:14:18.070
have built is actually
working as expected.

00:14:18.070 --> 00:14:21.840
Ability to push the system
into a previous state

00:14:21.840 --> 00:14:25.540
where you can look at and
process parts of the data

00:14:25.540 --> 00:14:28.790
to get a better understanding
of the functionality,

00:14:28.790 --> 00:14:31.892
whether it that has to, as
per your requirements or not,

00:14:31.892 --> 00:14:32.600
it's [INAUDIBLE].

00:14:32.600 --> 00:14:36.350
A functional test capability is
being provided as part of it.

00:14:36.350 --> 00:14:38.750
So operationally, I mean--

00:14:38.750 --> 00:14:40.940
and from a CI/CD
perspective, you

00:14:40.940 --> 00:14:43.280
should be still able to
support taking the pipelines

00:14:43.280 --> 00:14:46.310
that you have built and be
able to take all of that stuff

00:14:46.310 --> 00:14:50.330
and push it into a
CI/CD environment

00:14:50.330 --> 00:14:52.420
so that you can run automated
tests as part of it.

00:14:55.170 --> 00:14:58.430
So blending data from
various different sources--

00:14:58.430 --> 00:14:59.540
extremely important.

00:14:59.540 --> 00:15:02.400
So with Data Fusion,
as an example,

00:15:02.400 --> 00:15:04.900
it allows you to bring data
from various different sources.

00:15:04.900 --> 00:15:08.990
Whether you're bringing
data from RDBMS, warehouses,

00:15:08.990 --> 00:15:12.980
SAS sources, cloud,
mainframes, it doesn't matter,

00:15:12.980 --> 00:15:14.900
there are 200-plus
connectors that

00:15:14.900 --> 00:15:16.940
exist right now in
Data Fusion that allows

00:15:16.940 --> 00:15:18.810
you to bring this data in.

00:15:18.810 --> 00:15:23.210
Now all of this data
can be processed using

00:15:23.210 --> 00:15:24.500
various different systems.

00:15:24.500 --> 00:15:27.260
Data Fusion can basically
allow you to do that.

00:15:27.260 --> 00:15:29.450
But you can also use Dataproc.

00:15:29.450 --> 00:15:30.620
You can also use BigQuery.

00:15:30.620 --> 00:15:32.600
You can also use Dataflow.

00:15:32.600 --> 00:15:34.933
So Data Fusion, right now
when you think about it,

00:15:34.933 --> 00:15:36.600
from a processing
perspective, actually,

00:15:36.600 --> 00:15:38.450
it runs on DataProc, actually.

00:15:38.450 --> 00:15:41.060
So it basically uses DataProc
as a processing engine

00:15:41.060 --> 00:15:42.420
or a transformation engine.

00:15:42.420 --> 00:15:46.550
But in the future, it will
also use BigQuery and Dataflow

00:15:46.550 --> 00:15:48.820
[INAUDIBLE] all of these stuff.

00:15:48.820 --> 00:15:51.960
But [INAUDIBLE],, as part of
the integration solution,

00:15:51.960 --> 00:15:54.920
you have the ability to ingest
data from all of these sources,

00:15:54.920 --> 00:15:58.620
bringing that into
GCP seamlessly.

00:16:01.540 --> 00:16:06.000
So in order to accelerate
transformations for analytics,

00:16:06.000 --> 00:16:08.810
one of the important things
is the data delivery modes.

00:16:08.810 --> 00:16:12.900
Can we deliver the data in bulk,
batch, and a streaming fashion?

00:16:12.900 --> 00:16:14.680
Yes, of course, we can do that.

00:16:14.680 --> 00:16:17.065
So let's talk about Data Fusion.

00:16:17.065 --> 00:16:19.590
Data Fusion basically
allows you to--

00:16:19.590 --> 00:16:24.930
provides a delivery mode in bulk
and batch and also streaming.

00:16:24.930 --> 00:16:28.570
Most of these actually
run on DataProc.

00:16:28.570 --> 00:16:31.200
So if you are using
batch, it's [INAUDIBLE]

00:16:31.200 --> 00:16:32.600
running in batch mode.

00:16:32.600 --> 00:16:37.475
If it is [INAUDIBLE] it's
using Spark streaming.

00:16:37.475 --> 00:16:40.140
And in addition to that, you
can of course use DataProc.

00:16:40.140 --> 00:16:42.500
And DataProc provides
similar capabilities.

00:16:42.500 --> 00:16:45.810
But you will just not have
the top-level environment,

00:16:45.810 --> 00:16:48.560
which makes it easy for defining
all of these transformations.

00:16:48.560 --> 00:16:50.710
But you can, of course,
try [INAUDIBLE] in DataProc

00:16:50.710 --> 00:16:52.000
to be able to do that.

00:16:52.000 --> 00:16:54.450
The same thing exists from
a Dataflow perspective.

00:16:54.450 --> 00:16:57.000
Dataflow has Dataflow
templates, if you

00:16:57.000 --> 00:17:00.140
want to use as a
low-test environment

00:17:00.140 --> 00:17:01.720
to define these things.

00:17:01.720 --> 00:17:06.750
But you can also use Dataflow
bare bone with its own APIs

00:17:06.750 --> 00:17:09.760
to be able to apply all of these
transformations as part of it.

00:17:09.760 --> 00:17:13.800
Now the interesting part of
this is, Dataflow is actually

00:17:13.800 --> 00:17:15.930
really good about streaming.

00:17:15.930 --> 00:17:18.930
So it provides amazing
streaming capabilities

00:17:18.930 --> 00:17:21.450
to be able to pull all of the
data in a streaming fashion,

00:17:21.450 --> 00:17:24.480
do analysis on it, and be
able to write that, either

00:17:24.480 --> 00:17:28.890
into BigQuery or any other
targets that exist on GCP.

00:17:28.890 --> 00:17:30.540
Now Data Fusion is
the only component

00:17:30.540 --> 00:17:32.130
that provides the
visual environment

00:17:32.130 --> 00:17:35.910
for composing the pipelines.

00:17:35.910 --> 00:17:38.520
It basically provides an
easy-to-use interface,

00:17:38.520 --> 00:17:41.550
which allows you to cleanse,
transform, standardize,

00:17:41.550 --> 00:17:45.450
canonicalize, [INAUDIBLE],,
aggregate, join, group by.

00:17:45.450 --> 00:17:47.400
Such transformations
can be easily composed

00:17:47.400 --> 00:17:49.740
within Data Fusion.

00:17:49.740 --> 00:17:52.363
Now across all of
the tools, services

00:17:52.363 --> 00:17:54.030
that I talked about--
no, sorry, tools--

00:17:54.030 --> 00:17:55.740
services that I
talked about, that

00:17:55.740 --> 00:17:58.320
is the ability for gathering
operational insights.

00:17:58.320 --> 00:17:59.880
Based on the pipelines
that you run,

00:17:59.880 --> 00:18:01.380
there are a lot of
different metrics

00:18:01.380 --> 00:18:03.570
that are gathered at
various different stages.

00:18:03.570 --> 00:18:04.920
Data Fusion will provide that.

00:18:04.920 --> 00:18:06.630
Dataflow also provides that.

00:18:06.630 --> 00:18:08.130
So there is an
operational dashboard

00:18:08.130 --> 00:18:10.500
that can be looked at
in order to understand

00:18:10.500 --> 00:18:14.280
the performance of the pipeline
and tune them [INAUDIBLE]

00:18:14.280 --> 00:18:16.390
the processing over
a period of time.

00:18:16.390 --> 00:18:19.330
So some of these things
are available as part of it

00:18:19.330 --> 00:18:21.600
out of the box.

00:18:21.600 --> 00:18:25.620
So like I talked about,
while Data Fusion

00:18:25.620 --> 00:18:27.480
can be used from an
integration perspective,

00:18:27.480 --> 00:18:29.510
all of the processing
still happens on DataProc.

00:18:29.510 --> 00:18:32.430
But DataProc and Dataflow
can be used independently

00:18:32.430 --> 00:18:35.340
for building some of
these capabilities,

00:18:35.340 --> 00:18:38.640
if you would like to have
the nonvisual environment.

00:18:38.640 --> 00:18:44.150
So they act as a transformation
engine of the Data Fusion.

00:18:44.150 --> 00:18:45.630
But they can be
used independently

00:18:45.630 --> 00:18:47.520
for even doing the same tasks.

00:18:47.520 --> 00:18:51.810
So the main thing, the one
point that I want to make clear,

00:18:51.810 --> 00:18:54.420
is we have the capability
right now to turn Data Fusion

00:18:54.420 --> 00:18:57.220
pipelines into DataProc.

00:18:57.220 --> 00:19:00.280
The capability for running
it on Dataflow doesn't exist.

00:19:00.280 --> 00:19:02.670
So if you are
invested and if you

00:19:02.670 --> 00:19:06.780
would like to see Dataflow
integration happening with Data

00:19:06.780 --> 00:19:10.590
Fusion, please do send us.

00:19:10.590 --> 00:19:11.538
And let's talk.

00:19:11.538 --> 00:19:13.830
And let's talk further about
the use case that you have

00:19:13.830 --> 00:19:15.570
and how we can
essentially help you.

00:19:15.570 --> 00:19:17.910
Would love to get a lot
more feedback on this.

00:19:17.910 --> 00:19:20.850
So please do let us
know as soon as you can.

00:19:20.850 --> 00:19:23.840
And for orchestration
perspective, of course,

00:19:23.840 --> 00:19:25.590
our Composer is the best.

00:19:25.590 --> 00:19:30.460
And it allows you to orchestrate
not only just the Data Fusion

00:19:30.460 --> 00:19:30.960
pipelines.

00:19:30.960 --> 00:19:32.600
But you can
orchestrate pipelines

00:19:32.600 --> 00:19:34.180
and various different things.

00:19:34.180 --> 00:19:38.610
So from that, one thing to note
is there's a better integration

00:19:38.610 --> 00:19:41.610
between Composer and
Dataflow-- sorry--

00:19:41.610 --> 00:19:42.690
Data Fusion right now.

00:19:42.690 --> 00:19:45.690
So Composer workflows
have actions now

00:19:45.690 --> 00:19:48.805
that will allow you to
deploy, trigger, start, stop,

00:19:48.805 --> 00:19:49.680
those kind of things.

00:19:49.680 --> 00:19:55.790
Do the lifecycle management of
the pipelines for Data Fusion.

00:19:55.790 --> 00:19:58.475
So let's talk about metadata.

00:19:58.475 --> 00:19:59.850
So like I talked
about it before,

00:19:59.850 --> 00:20:02.600
metadata plays a very vital
role in the next generation data

00:20:02.600 --> 00:20:04.490
integration solution.

00:20:04.490 --> 00:20:07.400
Right now, the difference
between what exists today

00:20:07.400 --> 00:20:10.100
and what we need to
shoot for tomorrow

00:20:10.100 --> 00:20:13.910
is the activating the
metadata that we have exists,

00:20:13.910 --> 00:20:14.798
currently existing.

00:20:14.798 --> 00:20:16.590
There's a lot of metadata
that's collected.

00:20:16.590 --> 00:20:17.840
It's just sitting there.

00:20:17.840 --> 00:20:19.490
People can look at it.

00:20:19.490 --> 00:20:22.910
They feel good about
that they have metadata.

00:20:22.910 --> 00:20:24.530
But there is nothing
else more than

00:20:24.530 --> 00:20:27.600
that we are actually
doing with that metadata.

00:20:27.600 --> 00:20:30.020
So the next generation
data integration solution

00:20:30.020 --> 00:20:31.820
should be taking all
of that metadata,

00:20:31.820 --> 00:20:34.610
looking at it from
the different lens,

00:20:34.610 --> 00:20:37.760
and using that information for
building better integration

00:20:37.760 --> 00:20:38.750
solutions.

00:20:38.750 --> 00:20:41.090
So the first thing, the first
step for doing all of that

00:20:41.090 --> 00:20:47.090
is the ability to capture all
of that metadata automatically.

00:20:47.090 --> 00:20:48.740
Ability of capturing
technical business

00:20:48.740 --> 00:20:50.740
and operational metadata--
and business metadata

00:20:50.740 --> 00:20:53.150
might be a little bit difficult
because business metadata

00:20:53.150 --> 00:20:57.110
requires you to have a good
understanding of the business.

00:20:57.110 --> 00:20:59.300
But [INAUDIBLE] technical
and operational metadata

00:20:59.300 --> 00:21:02.810
can be automatically
captured as part of it.

00:21:02.810 --> 00:21:04.840
Now once you have
captured that metadata,

00:21:04.840 --> 00:21:07.430
the ability to take an
end-to-end view of that

00:21:07.430 --> 00:21:09.510
metadata, of the data--

00:21:09.510 --> 00:21:11.210
so looking at the
metadata, you are

00:21:11.210 --> 00:21:14.540
able to construct an
end-to-end view of it.

00:21:14.540 --> 00:21:18.020
You are able to understand
the data model better.

00:21:18.020 --> 00:21:20.120
You are able to profile
the data and get a better

00:21:20.120 --> 00:21:21.440
understanding of that data.

00:21:21.440 --> 00:21:23.570
All of that plays
a very vital role

00:21:23.570 --> 00:21:26.540
in establishing various
different relationships that

00:21:26.540 --> 00:21:28.370
can exist between the data sets.

00:21:28.370 --> 00:21:30.790
So this is something
that is important

00:21:30.790 --> 00:21:32.040
and that needs to be captured.

00:21:32.040 --> 00:21:33.460
And we do that today.

00:21:33.460 --> 00:21:38.060
So as part of Data Fusion, we
automatically build a pipeline.

00:21:38.060 --> 00:21:39.870
All metadata gets
captured automatically.

00:21:39.870 --> 00:21:42.010
When you essentially
a query in BigQuery,

00:21:42.010 --> 00:21:45.470
that metadata gets captured
into Data Catalog seamlessly.

00:21:45.470 --> 00:21:48.530
The third part of
this is I don't--

00:21:48.530 --> 00:21:51.482
in a real world, there will
be multiple data catalogs.

00:21:51.482 --> 00:21:52.940
I don't think we
can ever say there

00:21:52.940 --> 00:21:55.735
will be one single catalog
that everyone [INAUDIBLE]..

00:21:55.735 --> 00:21:57.110
I would love to
have that vision.

00:21:57.110 --> 00:22:00.560
And I know whoever the wants
that central data catalog will

00:22:00.560 --> 00:22:02.710
actually own the data at least.

00:22:02.710 --> 00:22:04.460
But the biggest benefit
that the customers

00:22:04.460 --> 00:22:06.920
will have with having a
centralized data catalog

00:22:06.920 --> 00:22:10.320
is the ability to optimize
their processing for cost,

00:22:10.320 --> 00:22:16.010
for execution, for SLAs,
for even identifying

00:22:16.010 --> 00:22:19.850
the best tool or best workload
and basically the best

00:22:19.850 --> 00:22:21.620
tool for that workload.

00:22:21.620 --> 00:22:23.590
So having that
ability is important.

00:22:23.590 --> 00:22:26.720
So now the thing is, in order
to support that kind of vision,

00:22:26.720 --> 00:22:30.720
you need to have the ability to
exchange data between catalogs.

00:22:30.720 --> 00:22:35.580
So there needs to be a
standard formats of exchange.

00:22:35.580 --> 00:22:37.000
Or we need to expose APIs.

00:22:37.000 --> 00:22:37.690
And we do.

00:22:37.690 --> 00:22:38.920
Data Catalog does.

00:22:38.920 --> 00:22:43.150
[INAUDIBLE] catalog also
exposes that metadata out

00:22:43.150 --> 00:22:44.970
for the consumer.

00:22:44.970 --> 00:22:48.640
But to be told, there is a lot
more work to be done there.

00:22:48.640 --> 00:22:51.650
But it does have an
API, which can be used.

00:22:51.650 --> 00:22:55.150
But it's not very
comprehensive here,

00:22:55.150 --> 00:22:57.800
from a Data Fusion perspective.

00:22:57.800 --> 00:23:01.180
So for people who don't know
what Data Catalog is, just

00:23:01.180 --> 00:23:06.100
to give a recap on it, Data
Catalog is a catalog solution.

00:23:06.100 --> 00:23:07.870
It's a metadata
system, which allows

00:23:07.870 --> 00:23:11.440
you to discover, manage, and
understand your data assets.

00:23:11.440 --> 00:23:15.340
So it's basically
provides the ability

00:23:15.340 --> 00:23:18.460
to index all of the
metadata that is aggregated

00:23:18.460 --> 00:23:21.947
and provides a discoverability,
like search capability,

00:23:21.947 --> 00:23:23.780
where you can search
it based on the labels.

00:23:23.780 --> 00:23:26.487
Or you can search it based
on the name, description.

00:23:26.487 --> 00:23:28.570
It also includes a free
text search as part of it.

00:23:34.218 --> 00:23:36.010
It's a foundational
layer for [INAUDIBLE]..

00:23:36.010 --> 00:23:37.720
It's integrated with DLP.

00:23:37.720 --> 00:23:39.640
And it's integrated with IAM.

00:23:39.640 --> 00:23:41.650
And for people who
don't know DLP,

00:23:41.650 --> 00:23:43.870
it's the data loss
prevention, which allows

00:23:43.870 --> 00:23:46.060
you to tag sensitive data.

00:23:46.060 --> 00:23:48.520
And then all that
sensitive data that

00:23:48.520 --> 00:23:50.470
gets tagged gets
[INAUDIBLE] up in Data

00:23:50.470 --> 00:23:54.280
Catalog, which can be then-- a
proper action can be associated

00:23:54.280 --> 00:23:58.210
with it once that particular
sensitive data has been

00:23:58.210 --> 00:24:00.472
identified from Data Catalog.

00:24:00.472 --> 00:24:04.680
Next, there is no
discussion that

00:24:04.680 --> 00:24:07.590
ever happens about
metadata which it does not

00:24:07.590 --> 00:24:09.450
involve data lineage.

00:24:09.450 --> 00:24:12.120
And data lineage is
a critical aspect

00:24:12.120 --> 00:24:13.720
of data integration solution.

00:24:13.720 --> 00:24:18.180
And one of the main reasons
for that is our customers

00:24:18.180 --> 00:24:20.580
would like to understand
the flow of the data.

00:24:20.580 --> 00:24:22.590
They would like
to understand how

00:24:22.590 --> 00:24:26.040
the data that is being prepared
for business was generated.

00:24:26.040 --> 00:24:27.450
Because if the
business decisions

00:24:27.450 --> 00:24:31.920
are based on the final
analytics data that was created,

00:24:31.920 --> 00:24:36.510
they would like to understand
how that data was generated.

00:24:36.510 --> 00:24:40.380
[INAUDIBLE] purposes from
compliance purposes, this

00:24:40.380 --> 00:24:42.810
is an essential and
a critical component

00:24:42.810 --> 00:24:44.700
that any data
integration solution

00:24:44.700 --> 00:24:46.560
should be able to support.

00:24:46.560 --> 00:24:48.960
Now what does data
lineage provide?

00:24:48.960 --> 00:24:50.457
It provides a
granular visibility

00:24:50.457 --> 00:24:51.540
into the flow of the data.

00:24:51.540 --> 00:24:55.170
When you look at this particular
picture here, if you see,

00:24:55.170 --> 00:24:57.450
there are a lot of different
fine-grained details

00:24:57.450 --> 00:24:58.530
that are being provided.

00:24:58.530 --> 00:25:00.210
It's the individual field level.

00:25:00.210 --> 00:25:01.680
The data is being captured.

00:25:01.680 --> 00:25:03.480
When you look at an
individual field,

00:25:03.480 --> 00:25:05.530
you have to understand
where it came from.

00:25:05.530 --> 00:25:08.490
You'll also have to understand
where it is going towards.

00:25:08.490 --> 00:25:10.710
So what is the
downstream processing

00:25:10.710 --> 00:25:13.830
that it actually even be doing?

00:25:13.830 --> 00:25:16.690
Now when you think about
it from that perspective,

00:25:16.690 --> 00:25:19.980
this also allows you to have
more of an operational view.

00:25:19.980 --> 00:25:22.650
So you are able to look
at it and say, you know,

00:25:22.650 --> 00:25:25.670
this particular data was created
from these three different data

00:25:25.670 --> 00:25:26.170
sets.

00:25:26.170 --> 00:25:29.010
And these are the few things
that we have transformed

00:25:29.010 --> 00:25:30.900
in order to create this
individual field that

00:25:30.900 --> 00:25:33.870
exists in the target data set.

00:25:33.870 --> 00:25:38.580
And that is something that
has to be captured seamlessly,

00:25:38.580 --> 00:25:40.680
without any human intervention.

00:25:40.680 --> 00:25:41.880
They have to be cataloged.

00:25:41.880 --> 00:25:45.420
They have to be presented as
a fact and then visualized.

00:25:45.420 --> 00:25:48.000
And this is something that is
being provided out of the box

00:25:48.000 --> 00:25:49.350
as part of the Data Fusion.

00:25:49.350 --> 00:25:51.360
So lineage also
allows you to have

00:25:51.360 --> 00:25:54.498
things like root cause analysis,
impact analysis, provenance.

00:25:54.498 --> 00:25:56.040
All of these kind
of things comes out

00:25:56.040 --> 00:25:57.875
of the box from
that perspective.

00:25:57.875 --> 00:25:59.250
And as part of a
data integration

00:25:59.250 --> 00:26:01.350
solution, [INAUDIBLE].

00:26:01.350 --> 00:26:04.650
And I have seen, and I
heard a customer says that.

00:26:04.650 --> 00:26:06.990
[INAUDIBLE] can be
a separate solution.

00:26:06.990 --> 00:26:10.290
But it doesn't matter because
the only way while you

00:26:10.290 --> 00:26:13.830
will generally see that lineage
is attached from an integration

00:26:13.830 --> 00:26:17.490
perspective is because
integration solutions allow

00:26:17.490 --> 00:26:20.250
you to capture the
transformations

00:26:20.250 --> 00:26:23.810
in a standard form, that you
are combining two columns

00:26:23.810 --> 00:26:27.980
or creating 10 columns
or filtering data.

00:26:27.980 --> 00:26:29.820
These standard
operations, essentially,

00:26:29.820 --> 00:26:31.950
have to be captured
in the standard way.

00:26:31.950 --> 00:26:34.860
And then they can be associated
with every individual field

00:26:34.860 --> 00:26:38.650
that exists as part of
the integration process.

00:26:38.650 --> 00:26:42.960
And then be able to visualize
them seamlessly as part of it.

00:26:42.960 --> 00:26:44.820
So you get a full depth here.

00:26:44.820 --> 00:26:47.340
You cannot just take any
data, write it in a C code,

00:26:47.340 --> 00:26:49.510
and then say, oh,
show me the lineage.

00:26:49.510 --> 00:26:50.680
It doesn't come for free.

00:26:50.680 --> 00:26:52.320
But there needs to
be done something--

00:26:52.320 --> 00:26:54.330
APIs that are provided
that basically

00:26:54.330 --> 00:26:57.407
have to be used for capturing
the metadata of the operations

00:26:57.407 --> 00:26:57.990
that they put.

00:26:57.990 --> 00:26:59.610
So think of it this way.

00:26:59.610 --> 00:27:02.610
You would have to
write the documentation

00:27:02.610 --> 00:27:04.230
for the operations
that you are doing.

00:27:04.230 --> 00:27:06.770
And then you have to
do the operations.

00:27:06.770 --> 00:27:08.820
That's exactly what
needs to be happening

00:27:08.820 --> 00:27:10.048
if you are not using--

00:27:10.048 --> 00:27:12.590
and like an integration solution
or a structured environment.

00:27:12.590 --> 00:27:14.630
And SQL is a very
structured environment.

00:27:14.630 --> 00:27:15.630
So that's fine.

00:27:15.630 --> 00:27:17.870
But being able to do it
in an ad hoc environment

00:27:17.870 --> 00:27:20.840
is extremely complex for humans.

00:27:20.840 --> 00:27:24.990
Next thing that is important is
openness, or ability to share

00:27:24.990 --> 00:27:25.490
[INAUDIBLE].

00:27:25.490 --> 00:27:27.175
Like I said,
catalog-to-catalog exchange

00:27:27.175 --> 00:27:28.920
is going to become more common.

00:27:28.920 --> 00:27:33.440
But I don't believe we'll have
one catalog that basically

00:27:33.440 --> 00:27:35.060
collects everything,
which is totally

00:27:35.060 --> 00:27:36.560
possible in one environment.

00:27:36.560 --> 00:27:41.060
But different choices, different
customer requirements--

00:27:41.060 --> 00:27:44.210
you might end up in a situation
where you will have to share.

00:27:44.210 --> 00:27:48.190
So with the Data Catalog, you
can use the APIs to do that.

00:27:48.190 --> 00:27:50.840
With Data Fusion, you can
similarly use APIs to do that.

00:27:50.840 --> 00:27:55.450
And Data Fusion APIs allows
you to extract [INAUDIBLE]..

00:27:55.450 --> 00:27:59.250
Data consistency--
very important.

00:27:59.250 --> 00:28:01.250
You have to be confident
in the decisions

00:28:01.250 --> 00:28:04.100
that you are making for your
business based on the data

00:28:04.100 --> 00:28:06.350
that you have integrated.

00:28:06.350 --> 00:28:08.660
So the first thing
you have to ensure

00:28:08.660 --> 00:28:11.853
is when someone builds data
quality rules or someone

00:28:11.853 --> 00:28:13.520
doing the transformations,
we are making

00:28:13.520 --> 00:28:15.990
it hard to make mistakes.

00:28:15.990 --> 00:28:18.660
So you have to have
the ability to--

00:28:18.660 --> 00:28:21.600
with existing,
predefined directives

00:28:21.600 --> 00:28:24.020
and things that exist, it
just makes it extremely

00:28:24.020 --> 00:28:25.620
easy for defining that.

00:28:25.620 --> 00:28:28.100
The next is ability
to profile data

00:28:28.100 --> 00:28:31.200
and identifying quality issues.

00:28:31.200 --> 00:28:33.140
In Data Fusion right
now, you have a profiler

00:28:33.140 --> 00:28:34.940
that you can include
in the pipeline that

00:28:34.940 --> 00:28:37.910
allows you to extract a
lot of different metadata

00:28:37.910 --> 00:28:41.120
that exists in--

00:28:41.120 --> 00:28:44.900
ability to extract a lot
of metrics from the data.

00:28:44.900 --> 00:28:48.680
So it can help identify
whether the data is--

00:28:48.680 --> 00:28:50.870
there is categorical
data that has

00:28:50.870 --> 00:28:53.690
a lot of different metrics,
quantitative data, and then

00:28:53.690 --> 00:28:54.350
Boolean.

00:28:54.350 --> 00:28:59.120
So you can essentially look
at different statistics

00:28:59.120 --> 00:29:01.520
being generated for
different categories of data.

00:29:01.520 --> 00:29:04.400
And then you can investigate
that to make better decisions

00:29:04.400 --> 00:29:06.020
based on the
statistics of the data.

00:29:06.020 --> 00:29:08.200
So you can at least help
identify the problems

00:29:08.200 --> 00:29:10.600
if there are any issues.

00:29:10.600 --> 00:29:14.620
Handling data drifts--
nothing is constant.

00:29:14.620 --> 00:29:15.610
Things keep changing.

00:29:15.610 --> 00:29:16.860
Data sets evolve.

00:29:16.860 --> 00:29:17.950
So things get added.

00:29:17.950 --> 00:29:19.120
Things get subtracted.

00:29:19.120 --> 00:29:20.980
Things get modified.

00:29:20.980 --> 00:29:25.210
So ability to first
detect those changes

00:29:25.210 --> 00:29:26.770
that happen in the system.

00:29:26.770 --> 00:29:29.830
Next-- ability to
take actions on them

00:29:29.830 --> 00:29:31.030
is critically important.

00:29:31.030 --> 00:29:34.385
With Data Fusion, we do provide
some of the capabilities

00:29:34.385 --> 00:29:35.730
in handling data drift.

00:29:35.730 --> 00:29:37.210
We are able to identify them.

00:29:37.210 --> 00:29:38.890
And we can take certain actions.

00:29:38.890 --> 00:29:40.900
Either you can drop
them, or essentially you

00:29:40.900 --> 00:29:46.370
can filter them off.

00:29:46.370 --> 00:29:49.270
But the thing that we don't
have yet is being able to--

00:29:49.270 --> 00:29:51.200
I mean, I wouldn't say that yet.

00:29:51.200 --> 00:29:54.280
But the ability to apply
the changes on the target

00:29:54.280 --> 00:29:57.460
is not consistently available
across different components

00:29:57.460 --> 00:29:57.970
of it.

00:29:57.970 --> 00:29:59.575
It always depends
on the target system

00:29:59.575 --> 00:30:00.980
to which this may be applied.

00:30:00.980 --> 00:30:04.540
So it is a case-by-case basis,
at least from a Data Fusion

00:30:04.540 --> 00:30:05.660
perspective.

00:30:05.660 --> 00:30:08.740
And when you're talking about
in a Dataflow or DataProc,

00:30:08.740 --> 00:30:11.050
you will have to implement
that [INAUDIBLE]..

00:30:11.050 --> 00:30:14.210
The next piece is
open, and hybrid,

00:30:14.210 --> 00:30:15.580
and multicloud environment.

00:30:15.580 --> 00:30:18.250
We believe in GCP that
having the ability

00:30:18.250 --> 00:30:21.040
to take our integration
solution and moving that

00:30:21.040 --> 00:30:24.335
into [INAUDIBLE] should
give you [INAUDIBLE],,

00:30:24.335 --> 00:30:25.960
should give customers
the [INAUDIBLE]..

00:30:25.960 --> 00:30:29.310
Having that ability to
do that exists today.

00:30:29.310 --> 00:30:32.700
Frankly, if you look
at it, Data Fusion

00:30:32.700 --> 00:30:35.920
is based on CDAP open
source, which came from Cask.

00:30:35.920 --> 00:30:38.530
So that allows you to
actually run it on-prem.

00:30:38.530 --> 00:30:39.640
You can run it on-prem.

00:30:39.640 --> 00:30:42.070
You run it on another cloud.

00:30:42.070 --> 00:30:44.690
When you think about it
from a Composer perspective,

00:30:44.690 --> 00:30:45.640
it's based on Airflow.

00:30:45.640 --> 00:30:47.212
So you can run Airflow on-prem.

00:30:47.212 --> 00:30:49.420
I'm not saying that this is
the best way of doing it.

00:30:52.305 --> 00:30:57.100
By next, we'll have more news
on what we are doing there.

00:30:57.100 --> 00:31:00.130
But the whole idea
here is you today

00:31:00.130 --> 00:31:03.550
have the ability to run
them in these environments.

00:31:03.550 --> 00:31:05.860
So you can deploy them in a
hybrid environment, another

00:31:05.860 --> 00:31:07.630
[INAUDIBLE].

00:31:07.630 --> 00:31:10.600
And when you think about it from
a Dataflow perspective, which

00:31:10.600 --> 00:31:12.550
provides streaming
capabilities, you

00:31:12.550 --> 00:31:15.400
have Apache Beam as an
[INAUDIBLE] and open source.

00:31:15.400 --> 00:31:18.340
And you can use
that in [INAUDIBLE]..

00:31:18.340 --> 00:31:22.750
Data protection-- this is
one of the important aspects

00:31:22.750 --> 00:31:25.150
of an integration solution.

00:31:25.150 --> 00:31:30.490
Securing the keys is the ability
to store the keys in a secured

00:31:30.490 --> 00:31:34.120
fashion because there are many
aspects of data integration

00:31:34.120 --> 00:31:36.600
that have to be stored
in a secured manner,

00:31:36.600 --> 00:31:38.860
whether it's
passwords, auth tokens,

00:31:38.860 --> 00:31:45.500
all these cannot be in plaintext
field within your pipeline.

00:31:45.500 --> 00:31:48.370
So you would have to
store them in the KMS.

00:31:48.370 --> 00:31:51.270
And when you run a job, the
people with the right access

00:31:51.270 --> 00:31:53.380
will have the permissions
to extract those keys

00:31:53.380 --> 00:31:55.040
and be able to run that job.

00:31:55.040 --> 00:31:58.840
So all of the solutions
integrate with KMS.

00:31:58.840 --> 00:32:00.970
The ability to mask,
redact, and encrypt

00:32:00.970 --> 00:32:03.670
is critically important.

00:32:03.670 --> 00:32:04.718
You might think of--

00:32:04.718 --> 00:32:06.760
we are not talking here
about masking, redacting,

00:32:06.760 --> 00:32:10.290
and encrypting all of the data
and all components of the data.

00:32:10.290 --> 00:32:12.990
Here, having the ability
to uniquely identify

00:32:12.990 --> 00:32:15.070
certain sensitive fields,
be able to mask them,

00:32:15.070 --> 00:32:18.172
redact them, or encrypt them
is a great capability to have.

00:32:18.172 --> 00:32:19.630
And any integration
solution should

00:32:19.630 --> 00:32:22.480
be able to provide that
kind of capability.

00:32:22.480 --> 00:32:28.240
Now for GCP, we have one of
the best technologies that

00:32:28.240 --> 00:32:31.810
is available through DLP that
provides us ability to mask,

00:32:31.810 --> 00:32:32.860
redact, and encrypt.

00:32:32.860 --> 00:32:36.370
So Data Fusion is
integrated with the DLP.

00:32:36.370 --> 00:32:38.362
A few configurations,
and off you go.

00:32:38.362 --> 00:32:40.070
You are able to encrypt
all of your data.

00:32:40.070 --> 00:32:41.890
You can redact it or encrypt it.

00:32:41.890 --> 00:32:44.590
You can do the same thing
with Dataflow and as well

00:32:44.590 --> 00:32:46.090
as DataProc.

00:32:46.090 --> 00:32:49.400
We take standards and
compliance as they

00:32:49.400 --> 00:32:50.947
are the most important
thing for us.

00:32:50.947 --> 00:32:53.280
Data protection is the most
important thing for overall,

00:32:53.280 --> 00:32:54.370
for GCP.

00:32:54.370 --> 00:32:56.222
But we spend a
huge amount of time

00:32:56.222 --> 00:32:58.180
in ensuring that we
[INAUDIBLE] to the industry

00:32:58.180 --> 00:33:01.238
standards for data
protection policies

00:33:01.238 --> 00:33:02.280
and be compliant with it.

00:33:02.280 --> 00:33:06.250
HIPAA, PCI/DSS, [INAUDIBLE]----
these are some of the things,

00:33:06.250 --> 00:33:08.580
from a compliance perspective,
that we [INAUDIBLE]..

00:33:08.580 --> 00:33:10.886
And there's a long
list of things

00:33:10.886 --> 00:33:16.180
that we basically
are in compliant of.

00:33:16.180 --> 00:33:19.760
Now when you look at it from
a data protection perspective,

00:33:19.760 --> 00:33:23.260
then you want to ingest
data from an on-prem system.

00:33:26.400 --> 00:33:28.970
You need to have the ability
to have access to that data.

00:33:28.970 --> 00:33:30.760
So in which case, you
don't want to expose

00:33:30.760 --> 00:33:33.220
all of your internal
systems to the cloud.

00:33:33.220 --> 00:33:37.230
You want to have much
more fine-grained control

00:33:37.230 --> 00:33:38.230
of these type of things.

00:33:38.230 --> 00:33:41.360
With Private IP, we do support
those kind of capability,

00:33:41.360 --> 00:33:44.200
where you can expose
within [INAUDIBLE],,

00:33:44.200 --> 00:33:48.370
within your IP range, you can
basically give access to that.

00:33:48.370 --> 00:33:50.680
Ability to encrypt
data at rest--

00:33:50.680 --> 00:33:52.840
when you think about it
from that perspective,

00:33:52.840 --> 00:33:54.743
we need to have--

00:33:54.743 --> 00:33:56.910
you're going to use the
customer-managed encryptions

00:33:56.910 --> 00:33:57.410
key.

00:33:57.410 --> 00:33:59.350
Any of these tools
should basically,

00:33:59.350 --> 00:34:00.995
as part of an
integration solution,

00:34:00.995 --> 00:34:04.675
should provide you the ability
to pass on the information,

00:34:04.675 --> 00:34:06.550
or the encryption key
that is being provided,

00:34:06.550 --> 00:34:08.889
to the underlying storage
system where the data actually

00:34:08.889 --> 00:34:09.679
get stored.

00:34:09.679 --> 00:34:11.659
And it's encrypted
using the key that has

00:34:11.659 --> 00:34:14.610
been specified by the customer.

00:34:14.610 --> 00:34:16.679
In addition to that,
the data exfiltration

00:34:16.679 --> 00:34:19.300
process-- it's a protection,
which is extremely important.

00:34:19.300 --> 00:34:21.540
You want certain regions
or certain regions

00:34:21.540 --> 00:34:24.159
within the [INAUDIBLE]
data that it should reside.

00:34:24.159 --> 00:34:26.610
So you're going to use VPC
controls [INAUDIBLE] parameters

00:34:26.610 --> 00:34:27.480
around that.

00:34:27.480 --> 00:34:30.309
That will allow you to
protect the region in which

00:34:30.309 --> 00:34:31.860
the data should be residing.

00:34:31.860 --> 00:34:34.440
And all of these
services that I talked--

00:34:34.440 --> 00:34:36.810
Data Fusion, Dataflow,
DataProc-- all

00:34:36.810 --> 00:34:39.260
provide that kind of capability.

00:34:39.260 --> 00:34:42.370
Now putting it all together,
here's the interesting thing.

00:34:42.370 --> 00:34:44.340
We talked about various
different services,

00:34:44.340 --> 00:34:47.199
various different
capabilities that exist.

00:34:47.199 --> 00:34:50.330
They need to be all put
together into a single account.

00:34:50.330 --> 00:34:52.080
And this is where the
Data Fusion activity

00:34:52.080 --> 00:34:56.020
is going to shape itself into.

00:34:56.020 --> 00:34:58.330
Where you will have
the ability, when

00:34:58.330 --> 00:35:01.740
we bring all of the capabilities
that we talked about,

00:35:01.740 --> 00:35:03.200
bar from Data Catalog.

00:35:03.200 --> 00:35:05.790
We talked about DataProc,
Dataflow, such kind of things.

00:35:05.790 --> 00:35:11.460
And push them into Data
Fusion as the face for it.

00:35:11.460 --> 00:35:13.290
While you can use all
of these capabilities

00:35:13.290 --> 00:35:16.230
individually, but from a
data integration solution,

00:35:16.230 --> 00:35:20.340
we are essentially bringing them
all together into this piece.

00:35:20.340 --> 00:35:22.860
So they're all the things
we'll be integration.

00:35:22.860 --> 00:35:26.160
So when you think about it
just from a future perspective,

00:35:26.160 --> 00:35:26.860
you will have--

00:35:29.400 --> 00:35:31.380
we already run it on DataProc.

00:35:31.380 --> 00:35:33.240
We already run on [INAUDIBLE].

00:35:33.240 --> 00:35:35.920
So [INAUDIBLE] integrate
with BigQuery and Dataflow.

00:35:35.920 --> 00:35:38.100
So the whole idea
here is depending

00:35:38.100 --> 00:35:39.830
on the pipeline,
the characteristics

00:35:39.830 --> 00:35:41.340
of the pipeline,
and the data that

00:35:41.340 --> 00:35:43.290
is associated with
the pipeline, you

00:35:43.290 --> 00:35:45.618
need to be able to
pick which environment

00:35:45.618 --> 00:35:47.160
or which transformation
engine should

00:35:47.160 --> 00:35:50.710
be used for processing that
segment up the pipeline.

00:35:50.710 --> 00:35:54.750
That basically can be providing
cost optimization [INAUDIBLE]

00:35:54.750 --> 00:35:55.350
optimization.

00:35:55.350 --> 00:35:57.930
These are the two things that
would be exposed to the user

00:35:57.930 --> 00:35:59.430
so that they can control it.

00:35:59.430 --> 00:36:01.510
Of course, as a
user, you wouldn't

00:36:01.510 --> 00:36:03.570
need to have the
ability to choose that.

00:36:03.570 --> 00:36:05.850
You would want to have this
engine to be the default.

00:36:05.850 --> 00:36:08.430
And you want to run an
entire pipeline for this.

00:36:08.430 --> 00:36:11.020
But having that ability
is extremely important.

00:36:11.020 --> 00:36:13.510
So that's one place where
we basically bring that all

00:36:13.510 --> 00:36:15.450
into this canvas.

00:36:15.450 --> 00:36:17.020
The second is, like
I talked about,

00:36:17.020 --> 00:36:19.480
metadata will play
a very vital role.

00:36:19.480 --> 00:36:21.540
So we will be
integrating, looking

00:36:21.540 --> 00:36:23.730
at integrating with
Data Catalog because it

00:36:23.730 --> 00:36:26.700
becomes a critical piece
from an integration solution

00:36:26.700 --> 00:36:27.270
perspective.

00:36:27.270 --> 00:36:30.000
You need to bring all of the
metadata that exists there

00:36:30.000 --> 00:36:32.670
as well as be able to
exchange the metadata that

00:36:32.670 --> 00:36:35.707
exists in the CBF catalog
with the Data Catalog.

00:36:35.707 --> 00:36:37.290
So they will be
working in conjunction

00:36:37.290 --> 00:36:40.290
to essentially activate
that metadata so that we

00:36:40.290 --> 00:36:43.440
can do better integrations,
provide smart mapping

00:36:43.440 --> 00:36:46.155
capabilities, provide the
ability to recommendations

00:36:46.155 --> 00:36:50.218
for things that have to
be defined from a pipeline

00:36:50.218 --> 00:36:52.010
perspective, or
transformation perspective,

00:36:52.010 --> 00:36:53.980
or standardization perspective.

00:36:53.980 --> 00:36:58.350
So all that gets put together
in a much more coherent way

00:36:58.350 --> 00:37:02.490
as part of [INAUDIBLE].

00:37:02.490 --> 00:37:06.530
So this is one thing that
I wanted to talk about.

00:37:06.530 --> 00:37:08.753
And I know many customers
have asked about it.

00:37:08.753 --> 00:37:10.170
And you might be
already wondering

00:37:10.170 --> 00:37:12.640
because we talked about
it in the previous Next.

00:37:12.640 --> 00:37:16.590
Replicator-- it is basically
a [INAUDIBLE] solution

00:37:16.590 --> 00:37:20.680
that basically we're working
towards as part of Data Fusion.

00:37:20.680 --> 00:37:23.670
So this is something that will
be available in Data Fusion.

00:37:23.670 --> 00:37:26.340
It's a new Data Fusion addon.

00:37:26.340 --> 00:37:27.930
We have introduced
these concepts

00:37:27.930 --> 00:37:30.810
of addon, which really can have
a single Data Fusion instance

00:37:30.810 --> 00:37:33.255
and add these on top
of it to accelerate

00:37:33.255 --> 00:37:38.640
the replication of data at
low latency in real time

00:37:38.640 --> 00:37:40.790
from operational
databases and to BigQuery.

00:37:40.790 --> 00:37:42.290
So we will be only
supporting target

00:37:42.290 --> 00:37:43.640
as a BigQuery for this year.

00:37:43.640 --> 00:37:45.140
But in the future,
we'll be changing

00:37:45.140 --> 00:37:47.710
that to include other
topics, too, as part of it.

00:37:47.710 --> 00:37:49.510
So it's a log-based replication.

00:37:49.510 --> 00:37:51.750
So it will support
different sources.

00:37:51.750 --> 00:37:56.580
And as we come closer,
we can talk [INAUDIBLE]..

00:37:56.580 --> 00:37:59.820
If there are certain sources
that you are interested in,

00:37:59.820 --> 00:38:01.130
please do come talk to us.

00:38:01.130 --> 00:38:03.810
We'll be more than happy
to understand your use case

00:38:03.810 --> 00:38:06.390
and prioritize that for you.

00:38:06.390 --> 00:38:09.180
So with that, I
think we have covered

00:38:09.180 --> 00:38:11.920
a lot of different aspects
of data integration.

00:38:11.920 --> 00:38:15.860
And I think I would like to
basically leave you with,

00:38:15.860 --> 00:38:17.390
where we are seeing
in the future

00:38:17.390 --> 00:38:19.860
and where we would be
heading in the future?

00:38:19.860 --> 00:38:22.790
When you combine all of those
different aspects that we

00:38:22.790 --> 00:38:26.570
talked about for an ideal data
integration solution to exist--

00:38:26.570 --> 00:38:31.120
this picture looks complex,
which actually is not.

00:38:31.120 --> 00:38:35.020
When you think about what
is the most important thing.

00:38:35.020 --> 00:38:36.857
[INAUDIBLE] we'll
always have data sources

00:38:36.857 --> 00:38:38.940
that will be coming from
various different aspects

00:38:38.940 --> 00:38:41.610
of the organization and
various different systems.

00:38:41.610 --> 00:38:43.800
That will generate
a lot of metadata.

00:38:43.800 --> 00:38:45.300
There are different
metadata types--

00:38:45.300 --> 00:38:47.828
technical, operational,
business, social, quality.

00:38:47.828 --> 00:38:49.620
There are a lot of
different metadata types

00:38:49.620 --> 00:38:50.850
that we'll be generating.

00:38:50.850 --> 00:38:53.670
Right now, all that metadata
that exists in systems.

00:38:53.670 --> 00:38:55.185
It exists in Catalog.

00:38:55.185 --> 00:38:57.600
It exists in Data
Fusion catalog.

00:38:57.600 --> 00:39:02.090
But all of that metadata
is providing definition

00:39:02.090 --> 00:39:04.760
for different entities, this
semantic meaning for it.

00:39:04.760 --> 00:39:07.200
And they are providing
relationships

00:39:07.200 --> 00:39:08.850
with different data assets.

00:39:08.850 --> 00:39:10.650
All of that is
passive right now.

00:39:10.650 --> 00:39:14.060
So you can take that and turn it
into an active metadata, where

00:39:14.060 --> 00:39:15.580
you activate that metadata.

00:39:15.580 --> 00:39:20.215
You basically use AI-ML method
to generate more insight.

00:39:20.215 --> 00:39:21.090
I'm abstracting here.

00:39:21.090 --> 00:39:23.100
I'm not trying to
get deeper into this.

00:39:23.100 --> 00:39:26.370
But you'll be able to
extract a lot of information

00:39:26.370 --> 00:39:28.940
from that method, which
we have actually seen it.

00:39:28.940 --> 00:39:32.148
It brings in a huge amount
of information from it.

00:39:32.148 --> 00:39:33.690
You look at the
operational metadata.

00:39:33.690 --> 00:39:35.730
You can essentially
look at different ways

00:39:35.730 --> 00:39:38.040
of optimizing your pipelines.

00:39:38.040 --> 00:39:39.480
You look at the utilization.

00:39:39.480 --> 00:39:41.310
You look at data quality.

00:39:41.310 --> 00:39:43.260
You get a lot of
different aspects

00:39:43.260 --> 00:39:45.660
that basically
come with metadata.

00:39:45.660 --> 00:39:49.410
All this can then be pushed
in from a smart integration

00:39:49.410 --> 00:39:51.240
perspective, which
will be, again,

00:39:51.240 --> 00:39:53.130
which will be
[INAUDIBLE] automated

00:39:53.130 --> 00:39:54.610
with the active metadata.

00:39:54.610 --> 00:39:58.010
So all that stuff gets combined
together in such a way.

00:39:58.010 --> 00:40:00.780
Like I talked about,
adaptive execution,

00:40:00.780 --> 00:40:03.785
that plays a very wide role
in the next generation, which

00:40:03.785 --> 00:40:04.910
is going to be [INAUDIBLE].

00:40:04.910 --> 00:40:09.120
And Data Fusion is ability
to run across these engines.

00:40:09.120 --> 00:40:11.910
So as a customer, you should
not be worrying about where

00:40:11.910 --> 00:40:13.170
actually your pipeline runs.

00:40:13.170 --> 00:40:15.340
Of course, you would
need to have controls

00:40:15.340 --> 00:40:16.450
in SLA and [INAUDIBLE].

00:40:16.450 --> 00:40:19.608
But rest all should be
taken care by automatically.

00:40:19.608 --> 00:40:21.525
Whether you run it
on-prem, whether you run it

00:40:21.525 --> 00:40:24.600
on cloud, where you run
it, it shouldn't matter.

00:40:24.600 --> 00:40:27.150
That's point A. Point B--
the parts of the pipeline

00:40:27.150 --> 00:40:29.183
should be able to select
the best engine that it

00:40:29.183 --> 00:40:30.058
should be running on.

00:40:30.058 --> 00:40:32.240
So all that combined
together provides

00:40:32.240 --> 00:40:34.470
an active, adaptive
execution environment.

00:40:34.470 --> 00:40:37.620
And on top of that, we have
to have the best orchestration

00:40:37.620 --> 00:40:40.290
typically that allows not
only just the Data Fusion

00:40:40.290 --> 00:40:43.590
pipelines but also going
wide across the [INAUDIBLE]

00:40:43.590 --> 00:40:46.655
for supporting the end-to-end
data integration needs.

00:40:46.655 --> 00:40:49.280
In addition to that, when I look
at the data operation security

00:40:49.280 --> 00:40:51.930
compliance, there's
pretty much table states.

00:40:51.930 --> 00:40:54.900
And the solutions on
top should basically

00:40:54.900 --> 00:40:57.390
start provide
[INAUDIBLE] for retail,

00:40:57.390 --> 00:41:01.920
for finance, for telcos,
for different industries.

00:41:01.920 --> 00:41:05.640
We should be able to have
people solutions that

00:41:05.640 --> 00:41:09.460
basically come out of the
box for our customers to use.

00:41:09.460 --> 00:41:13.710
So this is the view on the
future of how data integration

00:41:13.710 --> 00:41:15.400
would evolve into.

00:41:15.400 --> 00:41:19.450
And this is how Data Fusion
would evolve into, too.

00:41:19.450 --> 00:41:20.560
That's all.

00:41:20.560 --> 00:41:22.150
If you guys have
any questions, I'll

00:41:22.150 --> 00:41:24.160
be more than happy to take them.

00:41:24.160 --> 00:41:26.280
Thank you for your time.

